Overview of strftime(format,[t]) function

This function converts the struct_time instance t or a nine-element tuple t into a string. The first parameter named format is passed a string containing the desired format of the output string
Similar to the format operator for strings, the format string contains multiple placeholders that are replaced with the corresponding values in the result.

Characters      Meaning
%a              Local abbreviation for the name of the day of the week.
%A              Complete name of the day of the week in the local language.
%b              Local abbreviation for the name of the month.
%B              Complete name of the month in the local language.
%c              Format for appropiate date and time representation on the local platform.
%d              Number of the day in the current month. Results in a string of length 2 in range[01,31]
%H              Hour in 24-hour format. The result always has two digits and is in the range [00,23]
%I              Hour in 12-hour format. The result always has two digits and is in the range [01,12]
%j              Number of the day in the year. The result always has three digits and is in the range [001,366]
%m              Number of the month,consisting of two digits in the range [01,12].
%M              Minute as a number consisting of two digits. Is always in the range [00,59].
%p              Local equivalent for a.m. or p.m.
%S              Seconds as a number consisting of two digits. Is always in the range [00,61].
%U              Number of the current week in the year, with Sunday being considered the first day of the week. The result has two digits and is in the range [01,53]. The period at the beginning of the year before the first Sunday is defined as week 0.
%w              Number of the current day in the week. Sunday is considered day 0. The result is in range [0,6].
%W              like %U, except that instead of Sunday, Monday is considered day 0 of the week.
%x              Date format of the local platform.
%X              Time format of the local platform
%y              Year without century indication. The result always consists of two digits and is in the range [00,99].
%Y              Complete year with century indication.
%Z              Name of the local time zone, or an empty string if no local time zone has been set.
%%              Results in a percentage sing % in the result string. 

Example for strptime(string, [format])

You can use strptime to convert a time string back to a time.struch_time instance. The format parameter specifies the format in which the string contains the time. The structure of such format strings is the same as for strftime:

